That puts it just behind Major League Baseball as one of the largest spectator events in the country.

30
00:02:38,141 --> 00:02:42,940
It's the Indianapolis 500 and the Top Gun and the Fourth of July all rolled into one.

31
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:53,340
Back in 1903, the Wright brothers' historic first flight was received with far less enthusiasm.

32
00:02:54,920 --> 00:03:01,140
The reaction to the flight by the public and the media was not very much.

33
00:03:01,141 --> 00:03:11,220
In fact, the classic story is that one of the Kitty Hawk natives came into the post office at Kitty Hawk on December the 17th, 1903,

34
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:16,360
and announced to the few people in the post office, damn if they ain't flew.

35
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:26,140
By 1908, other inventors like motorcycle designer Glenn Curtis had developed their own versions of the state-of-the-art biplane.

36
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:37,480
They needed to have the two wings, top and bottom, and the bracing and strut in between them to make a good, strong structure.

37
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,460
And, of course, they thought that two wings would give more lift than one, and that was actually correct.

38
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:49,420
At this point in aviation, individuals were willing to try anything to see if it would work.

39
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:53,820
There was continual evolution between 1903 and 1909.

40
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:58,540
In 1908, the Wrights went public with their planes.

41
00:03:58,541 --> 00:04:04,220
flying for a record 2 minutes and 17 seconds in front of a crowd of French aviation enthusiasts.

42
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:13,040
France had led the aviation industry since the Montgolfier brothers' first balloon flight in 1783.

43
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:20,740
In 1909, France reclaimed its leadership by hosting the world's first air show, En Rance.

44
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:24,400
In what was dubbed the Great Week of the Champagne,

45
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:30,160
Glenn Curtis beat out three of the Wright brothers' planes to set the new world record for speed.

46
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:37,880
Less than a month later, the city of Paris launched the first Paris Air Show.

47
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:42,360
100,000 people flocked to the spectacular three-week exhibit.

48
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:48,760
America followed with its first air show in Dominguez Hills, California in 1910.

49
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:54,320
Aviation was only seven years old, but airplanes and air shows,

50
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:57,720
were already a hit.

51
00:04:57,721 --> 00:05:01,620
By 1911, Glenn Curtis had launched an exhibition team

52
00:05:01,621 --> 00:05:05,360
starring daring pilot Lincoln Beachy.

53
00:05:05,361 --> 00:05:09,280
Curtis hoped his air shows would stir up interest in his new aircraft

54
00:05:09,281 --> 00:05:15,340
and squash any competition from the Wright brothers.

55
00:05:15,341 --> 00:05:18,680
When the US entered World War I in 1917,

56
00:05:18,681 --> 00:05:20,480
Curtis had won.

57
00:05:20,481 --> 00:05:29,560
90% of America's pilots were trained on Curtis Aeroplane and Motor Company's signature JN-4 biplane, nicknamed the Jenny.

58
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:41,380
The engine was an OX-5 engine, which had about 90 horsepower, as compared to about 400 horsepower for the Havilland DH-4 that was the actual fighter airplane.

59
00:05:41,381 --> 00:05:50,700
The performance of the Jenny was marginal, but it was easy to repair and it flew very slowly, so when you crashed, you probably survived.

60
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:56,680
More than 8,000 Jennys ultimately were built for the U.S., Canada, and Great Britain.

61
00:05:57,340 --> 00:06:05,500
The plane never saw action during the war, but afterwards, it changed the face of aviation and reinvented the air show.

62
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:13,600
After World War I, the government made a lot of these Curtis Jennings available as surplus at just several hundred dollars apiece.

63
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:23,935
These pilots wanted to continue to fly, and the only way that was available to them at that time was to buy these jennies and take up people and give them rides around

64
00:06:23,936 --> 00:06:24,636
the town.

65
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:31,060
Most demonstrations were in farmers' fields, and there usually was a barn somewhere in the area.

66
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:33,360
And so they became known as barnstormers.

67
00:06:33,361 --> 00:06:40,360
Barnstorming brought air shows, however spontaneous and rudimentary, to the masses.

68
00:06:40,361 --> 00:06:50,360
As more and more pilots began competing for customers, aerobatics, or stunt flying, took off.

69
00:06:50,361 --> 00:06:56,360
Without safety gear or parachutes, legendary barnstormers like Ormer Locklear,

70
00:06:56,361 --> 00:06:58,300
Bessie Coleman,

71
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:04,000
and Lillian Boyer crisscrossed the country doing daredevil stunts.

72
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:09,140
Throughout the roaring 20s,

73
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:12,280
at any given time as many as 600 aviators

74
00:07:12,281 --> 00:07:15,060
could be seen staging their outrageous air shows.

75
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:21,620
The way we would work it, we'd find a nice field that looked okay,

76
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:26,240
and land, and talk to the farmer who owned the property,

77
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:29,300
and asked him if we could operate out of his field.

78
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:31,280
We lived pretty good.

79
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:32,960
We slept under the wing of the airplane,

80
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:35,840
and we built up an awful lot of time.

81
00:07:37,380 --> 00:07:38,760
Aviation was still a wonder,

82
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:40,960
and watching airplanes fly overhead

83
00:07:40,961 --> 00:07:44,260
was an incredible experience.

84
00:07:44,261 --> 00:07:49,100
Here were these airplanes doing rolls and climb and so forth

85
00:07:49,101 --> 00:07:51,600
that gave people an opportunity

86
00:07:51,601 --> 00:07:55,060
to get caught up in the romance of entertainment.

87
00:07:56,360 --> 00:08:04,760
By the mid-20s, seasoned air show performers were banding together to form even bigger spectacles, known as flying circuses.

88
00:08:05,820 --> 00:08:13,300
A flying circus has to do with a group of people that all decide to incorporate and go from place to place.

89
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:18,560
And they'd advertise that this so-and-so was the greatest in whatever he did.

90
00:08:20,380 --> 00:08:22,960
Flying circus performers didn't just fly.

91
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:25,960
They walked on wings blindfolded.

92
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:28,920
staged mock boxing matches mid-air,

93
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:32,940
and jumped from plane to plane.

94
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:38,800
Famous shows like the Gates Flying Circus drew as many as 30,000 spectators.

95
00:08:39,700 --> 00:08:43,360
Flying Circuses offered a thrilled public the chance to witness the impossible,

96
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:46,400
and sometimes, the tragically inevitable.

97
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:54,520
For all the Barnstormers during that period of time in the post-World War I era,

98
00:08:54,521 --> 00:09:03,620
there was essentially no regulation in terms of flight safety, flight locations, airplane safety, and so forth.

99
00:09:03,660 --> 00:09:06,180
It was not one of the world's safe things to do.

100
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:14,320
There were 85 barnstorming-related deaths and 126 injuries in 1923 alone.

101
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:19,160
With an average of 70 people a year dying in plane crashes by 1925,

102
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:22,920
in 1926, the government was ready to step in.

103
00:09:22,921 --> 00:09:31,360
The Air Commerce Act said that not only did pilots have to have licenses to fly, but that airplanes had to be certified to be safe to fly.

104
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:39,160
It was really the start of the imposition of the federal government into aviation.

105
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:47,600
One of the barnstormers was quoted as saying, my way of earning a living is over.

106
00:09:47,601 --> 00:09:51,640
Barnstorming was on the way out

